A model's quantities say what is uncertain; its **terms** say how. A term is a log-density, built
from the model's quantities by [operations](../operations/) like any other value, and the model's
density is the sum of its terms. This page describes terms, the probabilistic graph they make with
the quantities, and what a snapshot can say about it.

## A term

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/term.rs:16..27}
```

A term is added in one of two ways:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/term.rs:328..351}
```

`score` adds the term of the distribution a quantity was drawn from: its density, as a function of
the quantity it **scores** and the quantities it is **conditioned on**. `factor` adds a
**likelihood factor**, which scores nothing: the likelihood of data that is not a quantity of the
model. Either way, the log-density must be computed by an operation and be a `Real`. A term that
scores a quantity is a density against the
[reference measure](../spaces/#measures-and-chart-volumes) of the quantity's space, so a quantity
whose space has none cannot be scored, and is a parameter, which likelihood factors may still be
conditioned on.

A distribution that scores the quantities drawn from it, such as [the normal](../the-normal/),
implements `Distribution`, whose `score` adds its term at a quantity, with whatever sampler and
roles the distribution gives it:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/term.rs:313..326}
```

Most quantities are made and scored at once, so the scope has a function that does both: `sample`
makes a quantity at an address, scores it by the distribution, and returns it, and `sample_in` does
the same for a space given as a value. What they build is exactly what `quantity` and the
distribution's `score` build, and a refusal by either is recorded at the line that called them:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/scope.rs:349..373}
```

> [!DECISION] `sample` returns the quantity alone
> A quantity is scored at most once, so its term is found from it, by `Snapshot::scored_by`. A
> model that needs the term's handle, to bind a role of its own or give the term another sampler,
> makes the quantity and scores it in two statements. See
> [the plan of the sampling entry](../../archive/sampling-sugar/plan.md).

Which quantities a term involves is never declared. It is read off the
[compilation graph](../building/#the-compilation-graph): the quantities among the roots its
log-density is computed from. Two rules keep the model generative. A quantity is scored by at most
one term, the distribution it was drawn from. And a term is conditioned only on quantities made
before the one it scores, so the order in which quantities were made is an ancestral order, in
which each comes after everything its distribution depends on. A quantity no term scores is a
**parameter**, such as a sensor reading the model is driven by.

The same reading is open for any node: `Snapshot::reached_from` gives the quantities a node is
reached from, which for a term's log-density is what it involves, and for a node it binds, such as
a normal's scale, says whether the node moves with the free quantities of a query.

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/model.rs:353..358}
```

## An example

A vehicle at $x_0$ and then $x_1$ measures a landmark $m$ once from each position, as $z_0$ and
$z_1$. Its motion is driven by an inertial reading $\zeta$, and a second motion model is a
likelihood factor over $x_0$, $x_1$ and a scale $\sigma$:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/tests/queries.rs:31..57}
```

Five terms score the five quantities $x_0$, $m$, $x_1$, $z_0$ and $z_1$; $\zeta$ and $\sigma$ are
parameters; `t6` and `t7` are likelihood factors, the second an improper prior on $\sigma$.

## The probabilistic graph

The quantities and terms make the model's **probabilistic graph**. Its nodes are the quantities and
the terms; it has an edge from each quantity a term is conditioned on to the term, and from each
term to the quantity it scores. Every edge joins a quantity and a term, and the graph is acyclic,
by the rule above. For the example:

<ModelGraph spec={probabilistic} />

Inference reads this graph rather than being told about it. A snapshot answers the questions an
algorithm asks of it, among them the **Markov blanket** of some quantities, which is what a sampler
or a sliding window needs: the other quantities the same terms involve, so that the quantities'
conditional density, given everything else, is the sum of the terms they are joined to, up to a
constant. For the first tick of the example:

<Walkthrough spec={blanket}>

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/model.rs:274..317}
```

</Walkthrough>

The mathematics of all this is in the note on [generative models](../../math/generative-models/).

A term may also carry a **sampler**, which draws the quantity it scores, and **bindings**, which
mark its parts by role (a residual, a whitening factor) for the algorithms that read them. A
parameter may carry a **proposal**, which draws it when the model is generated.

> [!DECISION] Conditioning belongs to the query
> A term scores a quantity whether or not that quantity is later observed; which quantities are
> observed is part of each query. See
> [position 1 of the charter](../../archive/charter/writeup.md#1-a-model-is-the-joint-conditioning-belongs-to-the-query).

## Retirement

A model that runs indefinitely must forget: once some quantities stop mattering, they are removed,
and what their terms said about the rest is kept in a smaller form. That is a **retirement**. The
writer reads one for the quantities at some addresses, named one by one or as the namespaces they
lie under, with the values of those it fixes rather than integrates out:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/retire.rs:113..122}
```

Reading changes nothing. The plan says what the change will be: the retired quantities, the terms
joined to them, and their Markov blanket, the quantities those terms involve beyond the retired
ones:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/retire.rs:69..84}
```

The caller builds a **replacement** for those terms, a likelihood factor over the blanket that
stands in for their integral over what is retired. Its log-density is built in a scope the
retirement opens, from the plan, by the same functions as any term, and the retirement applies the
whole change in one snapshot:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/retire.rs:223..242}
```

The [normal](../the-normal/#as-a-likelihood-factor) builds a replacement in the
blanket's stacked tangent, and [linearization](../linearization/#a-retirement-s-replacement)
computes one from the retired terms themselves.

### What a retirement frees

Removing the retired quantities removes every value computed from them, but a value computed from
no quantity is not reached that way: a constant, or a value folded from constants. The scales of a
retired term's normal, its mean's anchor, and an earlier replacement's matrix and right-hand side
are all such values, and once their terms are gone nothing reads them. So a retirement also
**frees** every node it leaves needed by nothing: a node that is no quantity, has no consumer in the
[compilation graph](../building/#the-compilation-graph), and is held by nothing outside its edges,
which is to say it is no term's log-density and is named by no binding, no sampler and no proposal.
Freeing a node may leave its inputs with no consumer, and they are freed in turn.

Freeing is local. It visits only what the change touched: the inputs of the nodes removed, the nodes
the dropped terms and proposals named, and the nodes the replacement's scope made. A value that
nothing has read since it was built, and that the retirement never touched, stays. It changes no
value of the model: every term, sampler and proposal left reads only nodes that are left, so the
model's density, and every query of it, is what it would have been with the freed nodes kept. The
mathematics, and the proof of both, are in
[the entry that added it](../../archive/retired-constants/math.svx#freeing).

Freeing is what keeps a model that retires as it grows to a bounded size: its nodes are those its
current quantities, terms, samplers, proposals and pins need, with at most the values that no
retirement has touched since they were built. A window that keeps its
last few ticks holds a number of nodes that stops growing once it is full, as
[the sliding window](../../guide/sliding-window/#what-is-reported) checks. `Snapshot::nodes` lists
a snapshot's nodes, and `Snapshot::len` counts them.

### Pins

A handle names a node and is bound to no writer, so the model cannot know which handles a user
still holds, and holding one does not keep its node. A handle to a freed node fails as any handle
the model does not hold does, with `UnknownNode`, and never names another node, since identifiers
are never reused. Most nodes need no keeping, but some do: a constant made when the model is
configured, such as a measurement noise that every tick reads, may lose its last consumer at a
retirement that comes before the next tick is added. A node the user wants kept, whether or not
anything uses it, is **pinned**:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/scope.rs:455..487}
```

A pin keeps a node from being freed, not from being retired: a pinned value computed from a retired
quantity is removed with it. A released pin returns its node to the rule, and the next retirement
frees it if nothing else needs it, even a retirement that touches nothing near it. Pins are part
of the model, so a snapshot carries them, `Snapshot::is_pinned` reads them, and a branch forked
from a snapshot changes its own. In the retirement tests, a first tick whose measurement alone reads a
noise constant:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/tests/retirement.rs:1587..1611}
```

keeps the noise through the retirement of that tick when it is pinned, and a second tick reads it;
unpinned, the same retirement frees it, and the second tick's build fails:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/tests/retirement.rs:1622..1640}
```

> [!DECISION] A pin, rather than a counted handle
> Counting live handles would keep exactly what the user holds, but a counted handle can no longer
> be copied freely, and a forgotten copy would keep its node forever. Pins put the cost on the few
> nodes that need keeping. See
> [Handles the user holds](../../archive/retired-constants/math.svx#handles-the-user-holds).
